<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Graphical user interface (GUI) agents powered by large vision–language models (VLMs) have shown remarkable potential in automating digital tasks, highlighting the need for high-quality trajectory data to support effective agent training. Yet existing trajectory synthesis pipelines often yield agents that fail to generalize beyond simple interactions. We identify this limitation as stemming from the neglect of semantic-ambiguous actions—interactions whose meanings are context-dependent, sequentially dependent, or visually ambiguous. Such actions are crucial for real-world robustness but are under-represented and poorly processed in current datasets, leading to semantic misalignment between task instructions and execution. To address these issues, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>, a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H</span>ardness-<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>ware <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>rajectory <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>ynthesis framework designed to mitigate the impact of semantic ambiguity. We define hardness as the degree of semantic ambiguity associated with an action and develop two complementary modules: (1) a hardness-driven exploration that guides data collection toward ambiguous yet informative interactions, and (2) an alignment-guided refinement that iteratively validates and repairs instruction–execution alignment. The two modules operate in a closed-loop manner—exploration supplies refinement with challenging trajectories, while refinement feedback updates the hardness signal to guide future exploration. Extensive experiments show that agents trained with <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> consistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines across benchmark GUI environments.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="footnotex1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>Equal contribution <sup id="footnotex1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="footnotex1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup>Corresponding author</span></span></span>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig_1.drawio.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:651/821;" width="651" height="821" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of trajectory synthesis paradigms. Compared with (a) existing methods, (b) <span id="S0.F1.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> integrates hardness-driven exploration and alignment-guided refinement in a closed loop, producing high-quality trajectories with rich semantic coverage and strong instruction–execution alignment. (c) Experiments show <span id="S0.F1.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> outperforms <span id="S0.F1.13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> by <span id="S0.F1.13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100%↑</span> on <span id="S0.F1.13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> (<span id="S0.F1.13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">22.60 vs. 11.30</span>) and <span id="S0.F1.13.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">215%↑</span> on <span id="S0.F1.13.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span> (<span id="S0.F1.13.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20.60 vs. 6.53</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graphical User Interface</span> (GUI) agents powered by large <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision–Language Models</span> (VLMs) have emerged as a promising direction for automating complex digital workflows, from booking travel in mobile apps to configuring cloud services in browsers. A capable agent must perceive the screen, interpret a natural-language goal, decompose it into a plan, and execute actions such as <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tap</span>, <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Scroll</span>, or <span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Type</span>. Training such autonomy requires instruction-aligned trajectories coupling task goals, step-level descriptions, and exact action–state pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>. However, collecting such trajectories manually is prohibitively expensive, motivating recent efforts toward automated trajectory synthesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automating Trajectory Synthesis.</span>
To reduce human annotation, recent work automates trajectory generation. Early <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">task-driven</span> approaches begin with written goals and record agents or humans executing them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, but still depend on costly goal specification and yield inconsistent traces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> instead introduces <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reverse task synthesis</span>, where the system explores applications via random UI actions and then synthesizes task instructions to describe observed outcomes, improving diversity by removing manual goal authoring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Semantic-Ambiguity Gap.</span>
Despite these advantages, reverse task synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> still faces critical limitations.
Exploration based on uniform random walks or shallow breadth-first search causes over 70% of collected traces to collapse into trivial <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic-intuitive actions</span> (e.g., “open menu”, “tap back”), which quickly saturate learning yet dominate storage and training budgets.
Conversely, the resulting corpora remain deficient in <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic-ambiguous actions</span>—UI interactions whose functional meaning depends on subtle visual or contextual cues and are easily confused or hidden.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, these actions include (a) identical “plus” icons that trigger different functions depending on context, (b) operations that only succeed after prerequisite steps, and (c) visually similar elements leading to distinct outcomes.
Such rare yet crucial actions represent the key bottlenecks where agents fail to generalize: without adequate exposure, they plateau at superficial behaviors and cannot handle unseen workflows.
Moreover, when these actions do appear, their ambiguity often leads to <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">instruction–execution misalignment</span>: synthesized instructions may omit essential context or replay inconsistently, introducing noisy supervision that further degrades data quality. This issue is exacerbated by existing one-shot instruction generation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, which lack semantic validation and thus produce corpora that are both redundant and unreliable. The <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">challenge</span> is thus twofold: (i) how to focus exploration on high-value, semantically ambiguous regions of the state space rather than collecting redundant, intuitive actions; and (ii) how to ensure alignment between synthesized instructions and actual executions to prevent supervision noise. Existing one-shot instruction generation lacks semantic validation, making the resulting corpora both redundant and unreliable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig1.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/304;" width="685" height="304" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrative cases of semantic-ambiguous actions. Such actions constitute critical bottlenecks for robust agent generalization but are rarely captured by existing synthesis pipelines.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis (HATS).</span>
To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>, a closed-loop trajectory synthesis framework explicitly targeting semantic-ambiguous actions through two cooperative modules (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
The <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Exploration</span> module (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Hardness-Driven Exploration Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) steers search toward semantically complex, under-represented states,
while the <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment-Guided Refinement</span> module (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) performs multi-round replay and repair to ensure semantic consistency before data admission.
Both modules operate under a unified <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Monte Carlo Tree Search (HD-MCTS)</span> procedure,
which orchestrates exploration, refinement, and feedback propagation in a single control loop.
Exploration supplies challenging trajectories for validation, and refinement converts misalignment feedback into hardness signals that guide subsequent exploration.
This closed-loop design systematically enhances both the diversity and fidelity of synthesized data, yielding more reliable and generalizable GUI agents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig2_ours_overview.png" id="S1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/327;" width="651" height="327" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F3.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S1.F3.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S1.F3.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture of the HATS framework.</span>
The framework integrates a <span id="S1.F3.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Exploration</span> module (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Hardness-Driven Exploration Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and an <span id="S1.F3.13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment-Guided Refinement</span> module (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) within a unified <span id="S1.F3.13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HD-MCTS</span> loop.
Exploration corresponds to the <span id="S1.F3.13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection</span>, <span id="S1.F3.13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expansion</span>, and <span id="S1.F3.13.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Phase I</span>, while refinement handles <span id="S1.F3.13.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Phase II</span> and <span id="S1.F3.13.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backpropagation</span>.
Misalignment detected during refinement is converted into a hardness reward that guides subsequent exploration, forming a closed loop for progressively improving both diversity and semantic fidelity of synthesized trajectories.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span>
We summarize our contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I.</span> We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>, a unified closed-loop trajectory synthesis framework that enhances the representation and handling of semantic-ambiguous actions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II.</span> We design a hardness-driven exploration module that prioritizes rare, high-value actions while reducing redundancy from intuitive ones.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III.</span> We propose an alignment-guided refinement module that replaces one-shot synthesis with multi-round validation, ensuring instruction–execution alignment.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">These contributions collectively establish a scalable, semantically grounded pipeline that enhances diversity and fidelity in trajectory synthesis, advancing GUI agent training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GUI Agents.</span>
Recent advances in large language and vision–language models (LLMs/VLMs) have accelerated progress in agents that perceive, reason, and act within graphical user interfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>.
Earlier systems relied on prompt programming or external tool execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, while more recent work fine-tunes multimodal backbones to better recognize UI states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> and execute atomic actions across web, desktop, and mobile environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>.
Although these agents demonstrate strong generality, their performance remains heavily constrained by the quality and diversity of available trajectory data, especially when handling semantically complex interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GUI Trajectory Synthesis.</span>
A major challenge in data construction is obtaining large-scale, instruction-aligned trajectories that reflect realistic interface behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>.
Existing approaches follow two paradigms.
<span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Task-driven</span> demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> require manually authored goals and human/model executions, but are expensive and often yield inconsistent traces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
To improve scalability, <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> adopts an <span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exploration-driven</span> strategy, exploring UIs first and then synthesizing task instructions from observed outcomes.
However, naive exploration oversamples repetitive <span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic-intuitive actions</span> while underrepresenting <span id="S2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantic-ambiguous actions</span> whose functional meaning depends on contextual, sequential, or visual cues.
Moreover, one-shot reverse synthesis frequently produces underspecified instructions that fail to replay, leading to <span id="S2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">instruction-execution misalignment</span>. These limitations motivate our hardness-aware, closed-loop framework, which targets ambiguous interactions during exploration and verifies synthesized instructions through iterative alignment. Other contemporaneous efforts explore stochastic or intent-aware multi-app exploration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, which is largely orthogonal to our focus on addressing semantic ambiguity through hardness-aware sampling and alignment refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing automated pipelines struggle in two key ways: (i) they rarely capture semantic-ambiguous actions, i.e., interactions whose semantics depend on contextual, sequential, or visual cues; and (ii) even when such actions are captured, one-shot instruction generation often produces noisy or misaligned descriptions that degrade supervision quality.
Our framework <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> addresses both issues by tightly coupling targeted exploration with alignment-aware data verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method Overview.</span>
As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> consists of two interacting modules:
a <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Exploration</span> module that actively seeks semantically ambiguous, high-value actions (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Hardness-Driven Exploration Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and
an <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment-Guided Refinement</span> module that validates and repairs the resulting instruction–trajectory pairs before admitting them into the training corpus (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
These two modules are fused through a customized <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Monte Carlo Tree Search</span> (HD-MCTS) procedure that forms the unified control loop of our framework. Instead of treating exploration and supervision cleanup as two separate offline stages,
<span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> unifies them into a single iterative search process.
Each HD-MCTS iteration (Appendix Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ B.1.1 Environment Setup ‣ B.1 Evaluation Benchmarks ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) performs four coordinated stages:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection</span>: the agent traverses an action tree to balance exploitation and exploration using a UCB policy;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expansion</span>: the tree grows by executing previously unvisited actions and adding corresponding nodes;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation</span>: the agent rolls out a bounded-depth trajectory, reverse-synthesizes a natural-language instruction, replays and <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">iteratively refines</span> that instruction until it becomes executable and semantically aligned;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backpropagation</span>: an <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment-derived hardness reward</span> is propagated up the tree to bias future exploration toward semantically challenging regions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations.</span>
Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a GUI environment.
At time <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent observes a state <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (screenshot + optional UI tree), executes an action <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and transitions to <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}=\mathcal{E}(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}=\mathcal{E}(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The atomic action set is
<math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{\textsc{Tap},\textsc{Type},\textsc{Scroll},\textsc{Back},\textsc{Long\mbox{-}Press},\textsc{Swipe}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Tap</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Type</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Scroll</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Back</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Long</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">-</mtext><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Press</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Swipe</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{\textsc{Tap},\textsc{Type},\textsc{Scroll},\textsc{Back},\textsc{Long\mbox{-}Press},\textsc{Swipe}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A trajectory <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> records a sequence of interactions.
We represent <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">action tree</span>, where each node <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a state <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and each edge <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math> maintains a value estimate <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and visit count <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(v)=\sum_{a}N(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(v)=\sum_{a}N(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the current path (a sequence of <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,s)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs) with rollout depth limit <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig3.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/517;" width="685" height="517" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Comparison of exploration strategies.
<span id="S3.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform Random Exploration</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> often yields trivial and redundant actions, whereas our <span id="S3.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardness-Driven Exploration</span> replaces random walking with a hardness-driven exploration policy that selectively targets under-represented yet semantically challenging actions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Hardness-Driven Exploration Module</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">interaction-driven discovery paradigm</span> introduced by <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>,
where the agent explores by performing UI actions rather than following pre-defined goals. As contrasted in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, however, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> performs <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reverse task synthesis</span> from randomly sampled traces,
often yielding trivial and redundant actions.
In contrast, our approach replaces random walking with a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hardness-driven exploration policy</span> that selectively targets under-represented yet semantically challenging actions.
This module corresponds to the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expansion</span> phases, as well as <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Phase I</span> in HD-MCTS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I. Selection on the Action Tree.</span>
Starting from the root node <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with the initial GUI state <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
HD-MCTS traverses the action tree by recursively selecting outgoing edges using an Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) rule:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\star}(v)\;=\;\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}(v)}Q(v,a)+\;C\,\sqrt{\tfrac{\ln(N(v)+1)}{N(v,a)+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\star}(v)\;=\;\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}(v)}Q(v,a)+\;C\,\sqrt{\tfrac{\ln(N(v)+1)}{N(v,a)+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> controls exploration pressure. Unlike classical MCTS, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(v,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(v,a)</annotation></semantics></math> is updated via the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hardness reward</span> (Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.3 Closed-Loop Integration ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which we formally define later in the closed-loop integration stage.
This reward serves as an innovative bridge between exploration and semantic feedback—transforming alignment hardness into a guiding signal that steers future search toward more informative, ambiguous behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II. Expansion.</span>
If the current node <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not fully expanded, we select an unvisited action <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{e}\in\mathcal{A}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{e}\in\mathcal{A}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>,
apply it to obtain <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{e}=\mathcal{E}(s(v),a_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{e}=\mathcal{E}(s(v),a_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>, create a child node <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with state <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(u)=s_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(u)=s_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and append <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{e},s_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{e},s_{e})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This grows the search frontier along under-explored branches and increases coverage of semantically diverse UI states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III. Simulation Phase I: Rollout.</span>
After selection and expansion, we perform a bounded-depth <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rollout</span> from the current node by executing valid UI actions until <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> reaches depth <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\leftarrow\textsc{Rollout}(\mathcal{E},v,s,\mathcal{P},T_{\max})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rollout</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\leftarrow\textsc{Rollout}(\mathcal{E},v,s,\mathcal{P},T_{\max}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This process produces a concrete multi-step interaction trace, a candidate trajectory, which is then passed to the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment-guided refinement module</span> for subsequent processing.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig4.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/526;" width="685" height="526" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of instruction synthesis methods. <span id="S3.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One-Shot Instruction Generation</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> directly maps raw traces to text, often yielding vague or underspecified goals and inconsistent executions. In contrast, our <span id="S3.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Round Alignment-Guided Refinement</span> iteratively replays and verifies task instructions to produce semantically faithful and executable Verified Trajectories.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Alignment-Guided Refinement Module</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This module corresponds to <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Phase II</span> in HD-MCTS
(see Fig. <a href="#S1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
It receives noisy, partially meaningful <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration Sequences</span> from the previous stage
and transforms them into executable, instruction-aligned
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verified Trajectory</span>.
Unlike the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One-Shot Instruction Generation</span> paradigm in <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>,
which directly maps random traces to text,
our method employs a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Round Alignment-Guided Refinement</span> process
that iteratively replays, verifies, and corrects synthesized instructions
until semantic and functional consistency are both achieved (Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.1 Hardness-Driven Exploration Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt-Based Implementation.</span>
All stages of refinement that sub-trajectory selection, instruction synthesis,
execution replay, similarity verification, and contextual correction are
implemented via prompt-driven VLM modules <span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            The prompt templates used at each stage are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompt Summary ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I. Sub-Trajectory Selection.</span>
From the rolled-out <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration Sequence</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we first extract a semantically coherent segment:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leftarrow\textsc{SelectSubsequence}(\mathcal{P})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SelectSubsequence</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leftarrow\textsc{SelectSubsequence}(\mathcal{P}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">representing one meaningful user intent, such as “add a new clock”.
This selected segment is referred to as the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference Sequence</span>,
serves as the behavioral ground truth for synthesis and verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II. Instruction Synthesis.</span>
We then generate a natural-language description for the Reference Sequence via</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\leftarrow\textsc{SynthesizeInstruction}(A)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SynthesizeInstruction</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\leftarrow\textsc{SynthesizeInstruction}(A),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">yielding an initial hypothesized instruction.
Unlike <span id="S3.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span>, which treats this output as final,
we regard <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> as provisional and subject to replay-driven verification.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">
<span id="S3.T1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AndroidWorld evaluation results across task categories. All values are success rates (%). DLS = Daily Life &amp; Services; S&amp;C = Social &amp; Communication; S&amp;U = System &amp; Utility; P&amp;W = Productivity &amp; Work. The final column shows overall success rate.</span></span></figcaption>
