</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> on two representative GUI benchmarks:
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> and
<span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>,
which together span diverse mobile and web interaction patterns.
On <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span>, all 116 tasks are grouped into four functional categories,
<span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DLS</span>, <span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S&amp;C</span>, <span id="S4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S&amp;U</span>, and <span id="S4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P&amp;W</span>, defined by their interaction intent
(see caption of Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), with the full mapping provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Evaluation Benchmarks ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.
For <span id="S4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span>, we evaluate three lightweight yet representative domains (<span id="S4.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gitlab</span>, <span id="S4.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maps</span>, <span id="S4.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reddit</span>) to reduce computational cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, we use three VLM backbones
(InternVL2-4B/8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and Qwen2-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>),
with <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> for instruction synthesis and reward modeling.
All methods are trained under the same SFT setting with screenshots and
<span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a11ytree</span> inputs. We compare <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> against four representative data–synthesis
paradigms: <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Zero-Shot</span> (CoT + M3A) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Task-Driven
Generation</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Self-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>,
and <span id="S4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. <span id="S4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> uses the same 1K-trajectory synthesis budget as <span id="S4.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span>; the additional synthesis cost is bounded and tunable, with HD-MCTS controlled by the iteration budget and replay–refine capped by <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> with early stopping. Full training and efficiency details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Performance.<span id="footnotex10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnotex10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">2</span></span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Baseline reproduction uses available resources including official checkpoints, released data, and our own reimplementations (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_medium"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</span></span></span></span>
<a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> show that <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> achieves the
highest success rates across all backbones. Improvements are especially notable
in categories where existing methods perform poorly. For example, on
<span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S&amp;C</span>, <span id="S4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> reaches only <span id="S4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11.11%</span> on InternVL2-4B and <span id="S4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.00%</span> on Qwen2-VL-7B,
while <span id="S4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> attains <span id="S4.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">38.46%</span> and <span id="S4.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30.77%</span>. Similar gains appear on <span id="S4.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P&amp;W</span> (e.g.,
<span id="S4.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.85%</span> vs. <span id="S4.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.88%</span> on InternVL2-4B) and on <span id="S4.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span> domains such as <span id="S4.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gitlab</span>
(<span id="S4.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7.94%</span> vs. <span id="S4.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">22.73%</span>). During baseline reproduction, we also observed that adding <span id="S4.p3.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Self-Instruct</span> data sometimes degraded <span id="S4.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Task-Driven</span> performance due to instruction–execution misalignment and noisy supervision, further illustrating the sensitivity of GUI agents to data quality. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, the GPT-4o–based M3A agent is reported only as a reference baseline. These representative cases highlight a consistent pattern: <span id="S4.p3.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>
substantially boosts performance precisely in interaction regimes that existing
data-synthesis pipelines struggle with, leading to the highest overall success
rates on both mobile and web environments.
</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Component Analysis.<span id="footnotex12" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnotex12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">3</span></span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            To save computation, we evaluate on a representative subset of <span id="footnotex12.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span>;
see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Experimental Setup for Component Analysis ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_medium"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.</span></span></span></span> We next analyze the effectiveness of the three components of <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>: (i) the alignment-based hardness metric, (ii) hardness-driven exploration, and (iii) alignment-guided refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:129%;">Metric comparison of action-level alignment.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T4.7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.7.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Alignment Metric Definition in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:124.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.7.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Success Rate (%)</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.7.2" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;">
<td id="S4.T4.7.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tfrac{1}{|A_{a}|}\sum_{a\in A_{a}}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\exists\,b\in B_{a}:\,\mathrm{match}(a,b\,|\,s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF">[</mo><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">match</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" fence="false" lspace="0.170em" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;" mathbackground="#E6F3FF">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{|A_{a}|}\sum_{a\in A_{a}}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\exists\,b\in B_{a}:\,\mathrm{match}(a,b\,|\,s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.7.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:124.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F3FF;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.7.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">50.8</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.7.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.7.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T4.7.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tfrac{1}{|B_{a}|}\sum_{b\in B_{a}}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\exists\,a\in A_{a}:\,\mathrm{match}(a,b\,|\,s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">match</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">b</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{|B_{a}|}\sum_{b\in B_{a}}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\exists\,a\in A_{a}:\,\mathrm{match}(a,b\,|\,s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.7.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.8pt;padding-bottom:0.8pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:124.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.7.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.7.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">36.0</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis I. Hardness Metric Validation.</span>
We validate the hardness definition in Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.3 Closed-Loop Integration ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> through two complementary analyses: parameter sensitivity and metric substitution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter sensitivity.</span>
We vary <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.3 Closed-Loop Integration ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and observe consistent trends (Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3 Closed-Loop Integration ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Large <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> flattens hardness differences, while small or large <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> distort the reward landscape and reduce
discriminative power.
The default configuration (<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon{=}0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon{=}0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>) offers a balanced
scaling that preserves sensitivity to semantic hardness while maintaining
stable reward behavior.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig_7.png" id="S4.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/370;" width="685" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison on <span id="S4.F6.sf1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> with InternVL2-4B.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig_6_Baseline.png" id="S4.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/570;" width="685" height="570" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution: Baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig_6_HATS.png" id="S4.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/561;" width="685" height="561" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution: Ours</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison &amp; corpus composition.<span id="S4.F6.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Top: performance comparison on <span id="S4.F6.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> using InternVL2-4B.
Bottom: corpus composition by task category for the baseline (left) and </span>HATS<span id="S4.F6.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (right), where </span>HATS<span id="S4.F6.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> increases exploration in categories that are empirically harder for existing methods (e.g., </span>P&amp;W<span id="S4.F6.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">), while reducing exploration in easier
categories (e.g., </span>S&amp;U<span id="S4.F6.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">).
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric comparison.</span>
We substitute recall in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with an action-level precision
metric and derive an analogous hardness mapping. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, recall-based hardness correlates
substantially better with downstream performance: recall captures coverage of intended actions, which is the main failure mode in ambiguous GUI workflows; precision does not penalize missing steps.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/radar_chart_web_log_11_13_2.png" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/589;" width="685" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Anal. II: Action Type Distribution</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/radar_chart_multiround_refinement_11_13_2.png" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/589;" width="685" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Anal. III: Refinement Effect</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Radar chart comparison.</span>
(a) Action-type distribution on <span id="S4.F7.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span>, showing distributional differences
between the baseline and <span id="S4.F7.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span>.
(b) Per-task view of multi-round refinement, where the shaded area expands
across rounds, indicating higher action-level recall and better synthesized
trajectories.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis II. Effectiveness of Hardness-Driven Exploration.</span> We use two qualitative analyses to show that hardness-driven exploration
indeed shifts sampling coverage toward semantically valuable, ambiguity-rich regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distributional coverage analyses.</span>
Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 1st item ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>a reveals a clear pattern across
baselines: tasks in <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S&amp;U</span> exhibit uniformly high success rates, whereas
<span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P&amp;W</span> tasks show consistently lower success, suggesting that <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P&amp;W</span>
contains a larger fraction of semantically complex, multi-step workflows.
Motivated by this observation, we compare category proportions in the synthesized trajectories
(Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 1st item ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (b-c).
Relative to the baseline<span id="footnotex14" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                OS-Genesis (10/2025) does not provide category-labeled training data; we thus use <span id="footnotex14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Task-Driven w. Self-Instr.</span> for category-level comparison.</span></span></span>, <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> increases the sampling weight of <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P&amp;W</span> from
<span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">31.2%</span> to <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">46.6%</span>, while reducing <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S&amp;U</span> from <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30.8%</span> to <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">18.9%</span>.
This redistribution aligns with the empirical hardness indicated in
Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 1st item ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>a: harder categories receive more
coverage, while easier ones are deemphasized. A similar shift is observed in the action-type distributions on <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span>
(Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>a), which likely contributes to its improved performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantics-focused auditing.</span> We further compare whether the final trajectories synthesized by <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> contain
more semantic ambiguity than those produced by <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span>.
Using a third-party LLM auditor (<span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span>) following our rubric in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Experimental Setup for Component Analysis ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>, each trajectory is independently evaluated for three
ambiguity types: context dependency, sequential dependency, and visual
ambiguity.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, a consistently larger fraction of
<span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> trajectories receive ambiguity labels across all three categories,
indicating that hardness-driven exploration uncovers interactions where
language–action grounding is intrinsically challenging.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12138v1/Figure/fig_9.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/367;" width="651" height="367" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F8.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic-ambiguity ratios at log scale.</span>
<span id="S4.F8.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HATS</span> vs. <span id="S4.F8.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OS-Genesis</span> across ambiguity types
Vis, Seq, and Ctx on <span id="S4.F8.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AndroidWorld</span> (AW) and <span id="S4.F8.9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WebArena</span> (WA). Bars correspond to AW/WA-Ctx/Seq/Vis pairs.
Trajectories may involve multiple ambiguity types, so categories are not mutually exclusive.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis III. Effectiveness of Alignment-Guided Refinement.</span>
We evaluate how multi-round replay–refinement improves instruction–execution alignment.</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall effect.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that, refinement yields substantial gains:
the average action-level reconstruction recall <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.2 Alignment-Guided Refinement Module ‣ 3 Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
increases from <span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.26</span> to <span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.40</span>,
accompanied by a corresponding improvement in downstream success rate
(from <span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16.00%</span> to <span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">23.07%</span>).
This indicates that iterative replay increasingly corrects mismatches and
strengthens overall instruction fidelity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-case insight.</span>
A per-task view in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>b, using seven representative
tasks (Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Instruction Refinement Examples ‣ HATS: Hardness-Aware Trajectory Synthesis for GUI Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>), exhibits the same steadily improving trend:
the radar area expands monotonically across rounds, showing that iterative correction
progressively fills missing context, resolves sequential inconsistencies,
and produces more executable task instructions for individual behaviors.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
